# Line endings — one convention in the repository, on every machine.
#
# E15 (original): git's own checkout-time newline translation
# (`core.autocrlf=true` on this Windows checkout) is what re-introduces CRLF
# into the JSON manifests Prettier wants as LF
# (`apps/desktop/package.json`, `apps/desktop/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`)
# every time the tree is cloned or reset, even after
# `scripts/sync_version.py` and `make format` both agree on LF. `text=auto`
# lets git normalize line endings for anything it detects as text; the
# explicit `eol=lf` rules below pin the file types Prettier actually
# reformats, so that normalization never flips them back to CRLF.
#
# Widened when CI was added: the original rules named `.json`, `.ts`, `.tsx`
# and `.md` under `apps/desktop/`, which left `.css`, `.js` and `.html` out.
# Those are exactly the files `prettier --check` rejected on the first CI
# run — the same command passed locally, because the local working tree
# happened to hold LF for them while the committed blobs held CRLF. Naming
# the file types one at a time will keep producing that failure every time a
# new extension appears, so the rule is now the whole tree.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `eol=crlf` because every tool here either prefers LF
# (prettier) or follows the file (rustfmt, ruff), and because a GitHub
# Actions `run:` block needs LF to work at all. Windows tooling reads LF
# fine: MSVC, PowerShell, NSIS and the Tauri bundler all handle it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated rather than left to detection: a PowerShell or NSIS script whose
# line endings get mangled fails in ways that are hard to read.
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.nsh text eol=lf

# Binary — never diffed as text, never newline-rewritten. `text=auto`
# detects most of these on content, but a badly-detected binary cannot
# recover from a newline rewrite, so it is worth being explicit.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.pyd binary
